\documentclass{article}
\title{Stability of Perovskite Solar Cells under Humidity}
\author{Anon}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Perovskite solar cells reach high efficiencies but degrade quickly in humid air. We add a hydrophobic interlayer that slows moisture ingress.
\section{Results}
With the interlayer, cells retain 90 percent of their initial efficiency after 500 hours at 65 percent relative humidity, versus 60 percent for control devices. The interlayer adds one solution-processing step.
\section{Discussion}
The interlayer improves moisture stability without sacrificing efficiency. Its effect on thermal stability remains untested, and our aging tests stopped at 500 hours.
\end{document}
